WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.835
-Our next guest is an Academy
Award-nominated director.

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:04.637
His film "Postcard from Earth"

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:07.339
is showing exclusively
at The Sphere in Las Vegas.

00:00:07.339 --> 00:00:09.709
Please welcome to the show
Darren Aronofsky.

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:12.345
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:23.823 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:26.192
Thank you for being here.
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:29.862
I'm very excited to talk about
this technical marvel of a film,

00:00:29.862 --> 00:00:31.263
but I also want to start.

00:00:31.263 --> 00:00:33.766
The first time I became aware
of you was your film "Pi,"

00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:36.135
which came out in the late '90s,
which is a wonderful film.

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:37.336
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.336 --> 00:00:39.205
We just had
our 25th anniversary.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:40.907
-Just had the 25th.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:43.109
-You made this movie
for $60,000?

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:44.143
-Yeah. Pretty much.

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:47.813
-And so it was
a very lo-fi film.

00:00:47.813 --> 00:00:49.482
It was just, like, cut and run.
You made it.

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:50.783
But it was great storytelling,
and it was

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.386
the beginning of things.
And now this is so different,

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:54.754
what you did with this.
-How things change.

00:00:54.754 --> 00:00:55.855
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:59.425
So, The Sphere is in Las Vegas.
-Yeah.

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:01.427
Everyone's seen that thing.
-There you go.

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:03.229
-Yeah.
-And so when they first

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:06.265
approached you, like, what did
you know about this sphere?

00:01:06.265 --> 00:01:09.002
-I knew it was a crazy idea.
-Yeah.

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:11.537
-But I couldn't really imagine
what a TV screen

00:01:11.537 --> 00:01:14.540
four football fields large
would feel like.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.011
I actually didn't get to see it
till August of this year.

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:20.079
That was the first time
they turned it on.

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:22.615
And so we had been working
on these crazy images,

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.917
and then suddenly,
to see it everywhere,

00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:26.452
was a wild journey.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:30.790
-So, you filmed this movie
not quite being able to conceive

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.525
of what it would look
like when projected.

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:33.860
-It was a lot of guesswork.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.528
It was kind of shooting blind.

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:38.030
It was described
like flying the airplane

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.765
while building it,
and it was very much that.

00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:41.901
'Cause the technology
didn't quite exist

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:44.337
and how we were going
to actually interpret

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:45.605
all that data.

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:49.575
So, the camera outputs
32 gigs per second.

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:52.344
So like a movie on your
hard drive is three gigs.

00:01:52.344 --> 00:01:55.047
So this is 30 gigs --
32 gigs per second

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:56.382
to make an 18K image.

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:58.384
So the amount of information
was just --

00:01:58.384 --> 00:01:59.652
it was kind of mind-blowing.

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:01.420
-What was the size
of the camera?

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.990
[ Laughter ]
-So -- exactly.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.226
It took about
12 people to turn this camera on

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:09.062
because there was, like, fans
and overheating.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:11.831
And then, the final file
of the film

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:14.133
is what they call
a half a petabyte.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.935
-Okay.
-So that's after terabyte

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:17.436
is now a petabyte.

00:02:17.436 --> 00:02:19.072
I guess we'll all start...
-So, like, you

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:21.240
probably had to, like,
use Maildrop to send it.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.608
-Exactly, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:24.877
Re-transfer, please.

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:27.813
Yeah.
-And so I also want to --

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:30.015
So, the exterior of the --

00:02:30.015 --> 00:02:32.785
And again, I was seeing images
of The Sphere online,

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:35.054
and I didn't quite comprehend
what I was seeing, but...

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:37.457
-Yeah.
So this is actually a 2K image.

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:38.724
-Okay.
-So that's 2K.

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:40.527
We're 18K on the inside.

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:41.860
-All right.
So just to give a sense

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.030
of what you can do with 2K...
Like, that's --

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.298
I mean, if you see that
you realize --

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:46.532
-That's a real image.
-I know.

00:02:46.532 --> 00:02:48.200
And that's, like,
if you're in Vegas,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.203
you have a late night in Vegas,
and you wake up...

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:51.838
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:53.373
-It's true.

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:56.609
This is Summer League.
That's crazy.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.344
That is, uh...
-Yeah, that's --

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.779
that's a very popular one.
-That's a very popular one.

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:03.582
And this is,
U2 is doing a concert there.

00:03:03.582 --> 00:03:05.818
And then, the other...
-Attraction.

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:06.953
-Attraction.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:08.354
-Is your film.
-When U2's not there,

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:10.656
we're there.
Basically the thing.

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:12.425
-It's not the other way around?
-No, they got first dibs

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:13.526
on everything.
-Okay, good.

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:14.793
-It's good. All respect.

00:03:14.793 --> 00:03:16.028
-Yeah, yeah, very much so.

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:19.832
But, so the first time
the audience saw 18K...

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:21.366
-Yeah.
-...and somebody

00:03:21.366 --> 00:03:22.802
posted on Instagram.
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:25.571
-Can you talk about
the moment as you, a director --

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.840
You hadn't seen this technology
until recently, as well.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.408
What was it like?
-It was great.

00:03:29.408 --> 00:03:32.312
It was -- it was
what we were hoping for.

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:35.481
So, no one had ever seen it,

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.183
so we didn't really know
what they would respond.

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:40.520
And then this moment happened,
and it was just, like...

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.055
I don't know.
You always hope --

00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:45.057
I actually didn't know that
jaw-dropping was a real thing.

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:47.793
You hear that word, but it's fun
to look around,

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:50.196
and people are just like --
their jaws are literally --

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:51.797
literally dropped,
and they're all pointing

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:53.398
at different places
on the screen.

00:03:53.398 --> 00:03:55.901
So it's a joy to be --

00:03:55.901 --> 00:03:58.170
to kind of have audiences
go along for that trip.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:03:59.972
-Let's just take a look
at this short video

00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:03.376
just to see the reaction
to the first time seeing 18K.

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:08.915 line:0%
♪♪

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.616
Whoa!

00:04:10.616 --> 00:04:13.385
[ Audience exclaims, applause ]

00:04:13.385 --> 00:04:15.955
-This is crazy.

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:18.558 line:0%
♪♪

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:20.693
-Yeah.
-We have to stress, of course,

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.096
that's on someone's phone.
-Yes, exactly.

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.429
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.429 --> 00:04:27.233
-That's not what it's gonna
look like for you guys.

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.401
It's true. It's --
I was trying to shoot --

00:04:29.401 --> 00:04:32.571
I was lucky enough to post
the first images of it,

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:34.907
and I just --
I was sitting in that room

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.341
forever trying
to get a good image,

00:04:36.341 --> 00:04:38.677
and not to diss iPhone,
but it just wasn't...

00:04:38.677 --> 00:04:41.581
-No. Well, I mean, I guess
that's why it is an attraction

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:43.049
worth going to see,
because there's no way to see it

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:44.083
unless you're actually there
in person.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:45.751
-Yeah. And so --
and it's a strange thing

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:47.620
to try to tell people
what it's like,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.888
but it's -- it's kind of
a nature film

00:04:49.888 --> 00:04:53.659
where we shot in every continent
on the planet --

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:55.461
we went to the Antarctic,
we went to Australia,

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:58.997
we went everywhere -- and just
shot just the coolest stuff

00:04:58.997 --> 00:05:00.800
that you could imagine

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.170
but with a resolution
that's hard to sort of take in.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.405
And we also actually
shot micro images.

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:09.408
We shot -- there's one shot
of a praying mantis.

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:11.643
And most people, when you think
of a praying mantis,

00:05:11.643 --> 00:05:13.045
you're like with
a magnifying glass

00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:15.047
or you're really close,
staring at it.

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:19.952
But this is a 150-foot tall
praying mantis, fully in focus.

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.387
And so you could look at it --

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.689
And so it's not like
anything you've seen before.

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:25.657
It's pretty cool.
-Now, nature --

00:05:25.657 --> 00:05:26.859
You've actually done some

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:28.360
sort of nature filming
before this, as well.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:31.030
Was that something -- when
you first got into filmmaking,

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:33.632
is that a path you thought
you would maybe take?

00:05:33.632 --> 00:05:35.568
-I come from that background.

00:05:35.568 --> 00:05:38.171
I was originally trained
as a field biologist.

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:41.073
Strangely enough, I did a bunch
of research out in nature,

00:05:41.073 --> 00:05:42.441
and I've been kind of connected

00:05:42.441 --> 00:05:44.743
to lots of green groups
over the years.

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:47.780
And then, a really
smart executive

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.882
remembered that about my bio
and connected me

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:51.284
with a show called
"One Strange Rock,"

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:52.552
and it led to
"Welcome to Earth"

00:05:52.552 --> 00:05:54.987
and "Limitless"
and all this Nat Geo work I do.

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:56.788
So, when this came along,
I was --

00:05:56.788 --> 00:05:59.525
I was able to get
the best people in the business

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:01.293
to come work on it with us.

00:06:01.293 --> 00:06:03.262
-How did you find your way
into filmmaking?

00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:05.932
Was that something
that came to you in college?

00:06:05.932 --> 00:06:07.600
-Yeah.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.536
I went to college, and I
really didn't know how to write,

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:14.573
and my roommate was making
animation cartoons,

00:06:14.573 --> 00:06:16.442
and he was getting A's,
and I was getting B's,

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:18.044
and I was like,
"Oh, maybe I should start

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:21.246
taking drawing classes."
So that's how it started.

00:06:21.246 --> 00:06:23.382
-And when you go out
in nature,

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:25.651
especially, again,
when you have this camera

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:26.786
that no one's ever used
before...

00:06:26.786 --> 00:06:28.354
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:28.354 --> 00:06:30.723
-...I mean, are you just --
is it so thrilling to know

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:32.057
that you're going to find
the things

00:06:32.057 --> 00:06:34.760
that you would want people
to see for the first time?

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.262
-Yeah. It's hard.

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:38.831
It was a very -- it was
kind of a jigsaw puzzle in --

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:41.767
because the camera was
really unwieldy.

00:06:41.767 --> 00:06:43.035
It was very --
-I can't believe

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:45.271
the praying mantis, like,
stayed still for a shot.

00:06:45.271 --> 00:06:46.672
[ Laughter ]

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:49.508
-You know, I think you had
to shoot things that were --

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:52.545
that -- that held still, and,
like, a praying mantis,

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:54.079
after it eats something
sort of sits there

00:06:54.079 --> 00:06:56.148
for a while, digesting,
so that's when we shot it.

00:06:56.148 --> 00:06:57.216
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:58.817
But you look
for things like that.

00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:00.486
We tried to move the camera
a few times.

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:01.687
It's very tricky.

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:03.689
And then, we did a lot
of really cool stuff.

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:06.125
We attached it to a --
to a helicopter

00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:08.961
and flew it around for a while,
which was -- which was great

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:11.730
because I got to do that
with these incredible pilots.

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:13.031
And we were up in Oregon
when there was

00:07:13.031 --> 00:07:15.067
all these incredible
forest fires,

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:19.071
and we were just flying over
the most incredible landscape

00:07:19.071 --> 00:07:21.974
and just knowing
we were capturing it

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:24.509
with something that looked as
real as what we were seeing.

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:26.311
-So obviously, you love nature,

00:07:26.311 --> 00:07:27.547
you love being in the outdoors.

00:07:27.547 --> 00:07:29.248
How did you enjoy your time
in Las Vegas?

00:07:29.248 --> 00:07:31.450
[ Laughter ]

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:34.152
-There's a lot of -- a lot
of great talent comes through.

00:07:34.152 --> 00:07:36.988
I saw Adele twice,
so I can't -- I can't whine.

00:07:36.988 --> 00:07:40.760
It was an amazing show.
I saw Copperfield, I saw --

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:42.794
I just tried to take
in all the different things.

00:07:42.794 --> 00:07:44.630
-How much time did you have
to actually

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.533
technically spend
in Las Vegas?

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:50.503
-Too much, but I think it was --
But there is --

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:52.404
There's also the Pinball
Hall of Fame, which is --

00:07:52.404 --> 00:07:53.573
-Yeah. I have friends
who've gone

00:07:53.573 --> 00:07:54.607
to the Pinball Hall of Fame.
-It's amazing.

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:56.175
-Do you have
a favorite pinball game?

00:07:56.175 --> 00:07:57.777
-Oh, that's a good question.

00:07:57.777 --> 00:07:59.612
It depends what era.
What?

00:07:59.612 --> 00:08:02.648
But the old school,
it was a game called "Taxi,"

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.049
where you have to
pick up Gorbachev.

00:08:04.049 --> 00:08:07.219
-Yeah. Oh.
-Old school, you know.

00:08:07.219 --> 00:08:09.221
-He was in a --
-"Taxi" plus "Gorbachev" --

00:08:09.221 --> 00:08:11.357
I think we know when it's from.
-Exactly.

00:08:11.357 --> 00:08:13.292
And then --
But, you know,

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:15.327
I got into "The Twilight Zone"
game this last time.

00:08:15.327 --> 00:08:16.795
-"Medieval Madness"?
You ever play "Medieval..."

00:08:16.795 --> 00:08:17.829
-Love it, love it.
-That's a great game.

00:08:17.829 --> 00:08:19.231
-Great game, yeah.
-All right.

00:08:19.231 --> 00:08:21.534
I think when you tell people you
went to Vegas, and they're like,

00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:23.102
"What'd you do?"
you're like, "Pinball, baby,"

00:08:23.102 --> 00:08:26.705
they're like, "Oh. You're the
wrong person to go to Vegas."

00:08:26.705 --> 00:08:28.941
-I did have a run, though,
after --

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:32.644
So, like, after the premiere,
and people really liked it,

00:08:32.644 --> 00:08:34.246
and I was at the casino --
I don't gamble --

00:08:34.246 --> 00:08:36.015
and my friends
were at the table all losing,

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:37.316
and I was like, "You know what?
All this, like,

00:08:37.316 --> 00:08:39.084
positive energy is my way."

00:08:39.084 --> 00:08:40.186
And I did a run,
and I walked out

00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:41.787
with 800 bucks in my pocket.

00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:44.089
-800 bucks?
That's fantastic.

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:46.659
-Boom!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:46.659 --> 00:08:49.729
-That's how many --
That's how crazy the pixels are.

00:08:49.729 --> 00:08:52.197
There's more pixels than there
are money you won in Vegas.

00:08:52.197 --> 00:08:53.999
-Exactly, exactly.
-Hey, thanks so much

00:08:53.999 --> 00:08:55.300
for being here.
I really can't wait to

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.336
see it live.
Darren Aronofsky, everybody.

00:08:57.336 --> 00:08:59.238
"Postcard from Earth"
showing exclusive

00:08:59.238 --> 00:09:00.439
at The Sphere in Las Vegas.

00:09:00.439 --> 00:09:01.674
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

